# nu

> Generated by nushell_mcp learn() - server v{{ version }} (nu {{ nu_version }}). Regenerate when info().version differs.

The nushell_mcp tool interface plus the out-of-band conventions agents
need when authoring libraries.

## Quick example: glob directories via `run()`

Common starting pattern - list directories under a root via the
stateless `run()` worker. Demonstrates the args-record contract,
result_schema, body shape, and the lift-to-args lint discipline
(paths come in via `$args.root`, not as literal strings).

Nu equivalent:
```nu
# @returns record<dirs: list<string>>
def run [args: record<root: string, pattern: string>] {
    let g = (glob ($args.root | path join $args.pattern))
    let dirs = ($g
        | each {|p| { path: $p, type: ($p | path type) } }
        | where type == "dir"
        | get path)
    { dirs: $dirs }
}
```

MCP (partial):
```json
{
  "arguments": {
    "args_schema": { "root": "string", "pattern": "string" },
    "result_schema": { "dirs": ["string"] },
    "args": { "root": "/home/box/proj", "pattern": "*" },
    "body": "let g = (glob ($args.root | path join $args.pattern))\nlet dirs = ($g | each {|p| { path: $p, type: ($p | path type) } } | where type == \"dir\" | get path)\n{ dirs: $dirs }"
  }
}
```

Output (partial):
```json
{
  "result": {
    "structuredContent": {
      "result": { "dirs": ["/home/box/proj/sourcetrait", "/home/box/proj/shotbox"] }
    }
  }
}
```

## Schemas

`args_schema` and `result_schema` are structured JSON objects mapping
each field name to its type (item 21). The type vocabulary:

- scalar: a type-name string - one of `int`, `float`, `string`, `bool`,
  `datetime`, `duration`, `filesize`, `binary`, `range`, `number`,
  `glob`, `cell-path`, `path`, `directory`.
- record: a nested object, e.g. `{ "point": { "x": "int", "y": "int" } }`.
- table: an array holding ONE record, e.g.
  `[{ "name": "string", "age": "int" }]`.
- list: an array holding ONE element type, e.g. `["string"]`.
- oneof (type union): the reserved-key object
  `{ "oneof<>": [ <type>, ... ] }`. Members are recursive (any grammar
  type). Value nullability is the everyday case:
  `{ "oneof<>": ["int", null] }`.
- nothing: the JSON `null` literal (only as a `oneof<>` member).
- void: an empty object `{}` as the ENTIRE `args_schema` (a no-args
  function) or the ENTIRE `result_schema` (a no-value return). The MCP
  binds void args to `null`; passing a field to a void-args function is
  a type error.

Denied (each is one hard error at submit time): a nested empty `{}`, an
empty array `[]`, a table of empty records `[{}]`, an empty
`{ "oneof<>": [] }`, and `any`. Records and tables are OPEN (extra
fields accepted) but every DECLARED field is required and deep
type-checked - a `result_schema` must match what the body returns
exactly (see Nu Nuances: positional typecheck).

## Large payloads: shm IPC

The structured tool channel (args + the `result` envelope) has a
CLIENT-side size cap. An oversized result returns as an
`Error: result (N characters) exceeds...` envelope that spilled the data
to a file - that is the client truncating, NOT a nushell_mcp failure. For
a payload too big for the channel (either direction), pass it out-of-band
through a shared-memory file instead.

The MCP host, its workers, and your Read/Write tools share one
filesystem, so a file one writes another reads. The designated scratch
space is the tmpfs dir (`<identity>` = your agent identity):

```
/dev/shm/box/ai/<identity>/
```

The environment provides this dir (created at each login), so you do not
create it; it is tmpfs - its CONTENTS are transient (don't rely on shm
files across sessions). `/dev/` is on the hardcoded-path lint allowlist, so a
`/dev/shm/...` literal in a body does NOT trip the lint (still lift a
variable filename to args).

Large result OUT - write the data, return only the PATH:
```nu
# args_schema { shm_out: string }  result_schema { shm_out: string, rows: int }
let data = (ls /etc | select name size)        # stand-in for a big result
$data | to json | save -f $args.shm_out
{ shm_out: $args.shm_out, rows: ($data | length) }
```
The caller passes e.g. `shm_out: "/dev/shm/box/ai/<identity>/etc.json"`,
then reads that file with the Read tool. Large input IN: Write the data to
a shm file, pass its path (`shm_in`), and `open` it in the body.

Conventions:
- Name any arg/result field carrying a shm path with a `shm_` prefix or
  `_shm` suffix (`shm_out`, `in_shm`) - it flags the out-of-band intent.
- A shm file holds ANY format - NUON, JSON, YAML, raw text. Use whatever
  is smallest / easiest to parse on the far side (`to nuon` / `to json` /
  `to yaml` out; `open` or `open --raw | decode` back).

## Library authoring conventions

A library is a directory tree the agent owns and edits, promoted into
the MCP's signed canonical store by `commit`. The lifecycle is three
native tools over that source tree:

- `new` scaffolds - establishes the library, or adds a module / function
  skeleton.
- edit the scaffolded files in place.
- `commit` validates the whole tree and upserts it; `delete` drops it.

`call` then invokes a committed function; `info` / `inspect` are the
live index of what exists, each node's schemas, and its docs - read
those rather than tracking targets by hand.

### The function file: a single `main`

A callable function lives at `<module_path>/<name>.nu` and exports ONE
core decl - `main` - carrying an infix signature:

```nu
# <summary line, <= 80 chars>
#
# <optional details, any length>
export def main [args: record<x: int>]: nothing -> record<out: int> {
    # YOUR logic; the terminating record matches the output type
    { out: ($args.x * 2) }
}
```

- `main` IS the call-target: it holds your logic AND owns the whole body
  (no resolve passthrough, no call/main glue).
- The `[args: record<...>]` positional carries the ARGS schema - real
  fields, or `nothing` for a void (no-arg) function. A bare `record<>` is
  the unfleshed skeleton and is rejected.
- The `: nothing -> R` output type carries the RESULT schema - R a
  non-empty `record<...>` or `nothing`; an empty `record<>` is rejected.
- The positional runtime-enforces the args (missing / wrong-typed
  rejected, even on an any-typed value); the output type parse-checks a
  STATICALLY-typed result (a literal record). A dynamic / any-typed
  result is unchecked and records are open (extra fields pass) -
  best-effort result, reliably-enforced args.
- A call-target file MAY ALSO export helper `def` / `const`s beside
  `main`; only `main` is the indexed / callable target.
- `main` is a RESERVED word: it may appear ONLY as this exported
  call-target sentinel, never as any other def / module / directory /
  file / parameter / record-key / cell-path-member name in the library.

### Organizational (helper) files

A `.nu` file WITHOUT `main` is organizational: free
`export def` / `export const` helpers, no contract. It is not a
call-target (absent from `info`, not callable) - pull it into a function
file with `use ./<file>.nu`. A `mod.nu` may likewise carry
`export const` / `export def` shared utilities alongside its cascade.

### The mod.nu cascade

Each directory carries a `mod.nu` wiring its children:

- `export use ./<name>.nu` - re-export a sibling function / helper file.
- `export module <name>` - re-export a sibling subdirectory (which has
  its own `mod.nu`).

```
<library_root>/
  mod.nu              # export module geo
  geo/
    mod.nu            # export use ./area.nu
    area.nu          # export def main
```

### Node documentation + inference reduction

Every node can be documented: a FUNCTION via the comment block directly
above its `export def main`; a MODULE or the LIBRARY via its `mod.nu`
LEADING comment. The first blank comment line splits the block -
everything before it is the SUMMARY (<= 80 chars, the only hard rule;
surfaced by `info`), everything after is DETAILS (any length; surfaced
by `inspect`).

Write for INFERENCE REDUCTION. The reader already has, for free, the
- library name,
- module path,
- function name,
- argument field names (and types),
- result field names (and types),
- and the summaries of the enclosing library and modules.

Spend the summary on what those do NOT convey: units, side effects, what
counts as "valid", ordering / edge / failure behavior, the transform that
name-plus-types leaves ambiguous. Do not restate the coordinate or the
schema.

```
# coordinate: geo / shape / area    args {width, height}   result {area}

# noisy - every word is already inferable:
#   Computes the area of a shape from its width and height.

# lean - adds only what name + path + fields cannot:
#   Planar rectangle; result is in the inputs' unit, squared.
```

Details then carry the rest - the precise contract, gotchas, an example -
whatever a caller needs once the summary has told them whether they care.

### Where libraries live

- Harness-level (one agent's own knowledgebase): `./nu/<name>/` under the
  harness; the directory name equals the library name.
- Distributed (vendor-shipped, multi-agent reusable): a symlink
  `~/.sys/local/lib/nu/<vendor>/<name>` -> the project's library source tree
  (someday `/usr/local/lib/nu/...` when distribution-packaged). The
  `nu/` segment is the asset-type.

`new`'s establishing call records the source_path; `commit` always
re-reads through it, so you edit that tree and commit to promote.

### The lifecycle tools

- `new(library, source_path?, module_path?, name?)` - scaffold. The
  FIRST call for a name ESTABLISHES the library (source_path required
  then, immutable after). Later calls add a module directory or a
  single-`main` function skeleton. Additive: it refuses to scaffold
  over an existing leaf.
- `commit(library)` - validate the source tree (structure + the
  single-`main` contract + reserved terms + the summary-length rule)
  and upsert it into the signed canonical store, rebuilding the generated
  index + docs. Idempotent; returns the changed paths grouped as
  `{added, modified, removed}`.
- `delete(library, source_path, mcp_only?)` - guarded drop; re-pass
  source_path (matched by plain string). Removes the agent source too
  unless `mcp_only`.

`commit` rejects with `library::violations` (capped, truncation-flagged)
on: a malformed function contract, a `mod.nu` carrying anything but the
allowed exports, reserved-term misuse, a parse error, a bare `record<>`
skeleton, or a summary over 80 chars. Authored bodies are NOT
body-linted - you write them with intent; the hardcoded-path /
denied-external lint below applies to the `run()` / `interact()` surface
only.

## Lint

`run()` and `interact()` bodies go through an AST lint that hard-throws
on two violation classes BEFORE the body is evaluated; the rejection
arrives as a `lint::violations` error in the standard envelope. (Library
files promoted via `commit` are validated separately - structure +
contract, above - and are not body-linted.)

### Hardcoded paths

Quoted string literals starting with `/`, `~/`, `./`, or `../`
flag `hardcoded_variable`. Allowlist (prefix match): `/etc/`,
`/dev/`, `/proc/`, `/sys/`.

The rule fires on the STRING PREFIX, not on path-likeness or
length. A bare `"/"` separator trips it the same as a full
`/home/box/...` path. Lift anything that starts with any of the
four prefixes to args, even single-character literals used as
glue.

Bad - lint fires:
```nu
cd "/home/box/proj/example"
let p = "~/tmp/output.txt"
open "./scripts/run.nu"
let sep = "/"                                  # bare 1-char trips too
let parts = ($name | str replace "/" "::")     # `str replace`'s second arg
```

Error envelope:
```json
{
  "error": {
    "kind": "lint::violations",
    "data": {
      "violations": [
        { "kind": "hardcoded_variable", "position": [1, 4] }
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

`position` is `[line, column]` (1-indexed) of the offending
literal in the submitted body.

Good - lift to args (every offender, even the 1-char glue):
```nu
# args_schema: { "proj_path": "string", "out_path": "string", "script_path": "string", "sep": "string" }
cd $args.proj_path
let p = $args.out_path
open $args.script_path
let sep = $args.sep
let parts = ($name | str replace $args.sep "::")
```

### Denied externals

These external command names trigger `denied_command` when
invoked with the `^` external-prefix:

```
awk bash cp date echo find grep jq ls nu rg rm sed sh sort which
```

Bad - lint fires:
```nu
^grep "pattern" file.txt
^rg "needle"
^ls /tmp
^cp src dst
```

Error envelope:
```json
{
  "error": {
    "kind": "lint::violations",
    "data": {
      "violations": [
        { "kind": "denied_command", "position": [1, 1] }
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

Good - most denied names are also nushell builtins; drop the
`^` to invoke the builtin. For text search, use builtins or
`^python3`:

```nu
ls /etc                                       # builtin ls (no ^)
cp $src $dst                                  # builtin cp
open f.txt | lines | where {|l| $l | str contains "pattern"}
glob "*.rs"                                   # for find
^python3 -c "..."                             # for complex parsing
```

External commands NOT on the denylist pass freely:
`git`, `cargo`, `python3`, `tree-sitter`, `fd`, `ast-grep`,
`claude`, etc.

## Nu Nuances

### `path exists` follows symlinks - dangling links read absent

A symlink whose target is gone makes `path exists` return `false`
(it stats the TARGET), so an exists-guarded `rm` silently skips the
dangling link and the following `ln -s` fails with "File exists".
Guard symlink removal on `path type`, which lstats the link itself.

Bad - skips the dangling link, then ln fails:
```nu
if ($link | path exists) { rm $link }    # false for dangling links
^ln -s $target $link                     # Error: File exists
```

Good - type-guard (returns "symlink" even when dangling):
```nu
if (($link | path type) == "symlink") { rm $link }
^ln -s $target $link
```

Nushell language traps that surface as MCP errors when authoring
`run()` / `interact()` / library bodies. These are LANGUAGE
artifacts (not nushell_mcp surface), but they show up through the
MCP because the agent submits nu code via it. Listed here so the
patterns don't have to be relearned each session.

### positional typecheck is deep; records/tables are open; empties bind

A typed positional - the synthesized `[args: A]` on `__run` / `__interact`
and the committed `main` - checks the value's structure to its FULL depth
at RUNTIME; a wrong nested type fails `Can't convert to <full type>`. The
`: nothing -> R` output type only PARSE-checks a static (literal) result -
best-effort, not a runtime check. The structural rules:

- DEEP: declare nested types exactly. `table<c: int>` rejects a row
  `{c: "s"}`.
- OPEN: records/tables accept EXTRA fields/columns, but every DECLARED
  field must be present + correctly typed. Declare exactly what the body
  returns.
- EMPTY binds to anything: `[]` satisfies any `list<T>` / `table<C>`
  (no per-column check on an empty), so sometimes-empty result lists are
  safe under concrete types.
- `table<C>` and `list<record<C>>` are interchangeable at the checker
  and emit the same `[{...}]` JSON; prefer `table<...>` for a
  list-of-records.

### `else` must stay on the closing-brace line

`else` continues an `if` statement only when it follows the
if-block's `}` on the SAME line. A line-leading `else` parses as an
EXTERNAL COMMAND named `else`, so the body fails at eval - as
`worker::returned_error: eval: External command failed` (or the
opaque `Eval block failed with pipeline input` when nested) with
EMPTY cached stdout/stderr. Nothing points at `else`; suspect this
trap whenever those errors appear with no diagnostic.

Bad - eval error, no diagnostic:
```nu
if ($l | str starts-with "## 5.") { $in_s5 = true }
else if ($l | str starts-with "## ") { $in_s5 = false }
```

Good - `else` rides the same statement:
```nu
if ($l | str starts-with "## 5.") {
    $in_s5 = true
} else if ($l | str starts-with "## ") {
    $in_s5 = false
}
```

### Reserved keywords can't be bare record field names

Nushell keywords (`match`, `if`, `else`, `let`, `mut`, `const`,
`def`, `for`, `while`, `loop`, `break`, `continue`, `return`,
`try`, `catch`, `do`, `where`, `use`, `source`, `module`,
`export`, ...) are reserved at parse time. Using one as a bare
key in a record literal causes a parse error that surfaces
through the MCP as `worker::returned_error` with a parse-error
message naming the keyword.

Bad - parse error on `match`:
```nu
$items | each {|x| { id: $x.id, match: true, count: $x.n } }
```

Good - rename or quote the key:
```nu
$items | each {|x| { id: $x.id, matched: true, count: $x.n } }
# or
$items | each {|x| { id: $x.id, "match": true, count: $x.n } }
```

### `where` needs an explicit closure, not bare `$it`

Recent nushell tightened `where`'s bare-expression form. The
common pattern `where ($it | str contains "x")` on a list of
strings fails with `worker::returned_error: eval: Input type not
supported.` Use the explicit closure form with a named parameter.

Bad - "Input type not supported":
```nu
$lines | where ($it | str contains "knowledge")
$lines | where ($it | str starts-with "- ")
```

Good - explicit closure:
```nu
$lines | where {|l| $l | str contains "knowledge" }
$lines | where {|l| $l | str starts-with "- " }
```

### `open` auto-parses known extensions; use `--raw | decode` for text

`open file.md`, `open file.json`, `open file.toml`, etc. return
structured values (table / record / parsed-json), not raw strings.
Downstream `| lines`, `| str contains`, etc. then fail with
`worker::returned_error: eval: Input type not supported.` For raw
text, always force `open --raw | decode` first.

Bad - downstream operations fail:
```nu
let raw = (open ($args.path | path join "orientation.md"))
let n = ($raw | str length)
let pick_lines = ($raw | lines | first 5)
```

Good - force raw bytes then decode to string:
```nu
let raw = (open --raw ($args.path | path join "orientation.md") | decode)
let n = ($raw | str length)
let pick_lines = ($raw | lines | first 5)
```

`decode` defaults to UTF-8; pass an encoding string for others.

### `source` / `use` paths must be parse-time constants

`source $path` and `use $path` resolve their path at parse time;
runtime variables (including `$args.field`) fail with
`Error: nu::shell::not_a_constant`. The body must either inline
the logic or use a parse-time `const` literal. The literal still
runs through the hardcoded-path lint, so library-style
indirection through `$args.script_path` is structurally
impossible from a `run()` body - inline the function or use
`call()` against a committed library instead.

Bad - "Not a constant":
```nu
source $args.script_path        # runtime variable -> parse error
use $args.module_path *         # same
```

Good - inline the logic OR author the function as a library + `call` it:
```nu
# inline option: paste the function body into the run() body directly

# library option: author the .nu via new + commit, then call() it
#   (discover the target + its schema via info() / inspect())
mcp__nushell__call --library mylib --module_path foo --name bar --args { ... }
```

### `str join` separator is a value, not a regex / format string

`str join` takes a SEPARATOR VALUE. `\n` inside a double-quoted
literal IS a newline (nushell interprets the escape), so
`str join "\n"` works as expected. But for clarity and to avoid
confusion with regex / format-string contexts, prefer
`(char nl)` for newlines, `(char tab)` for tabs, etc. These are
const-fn lookups and parse cleanly in all contexts.

Style preference:
```nu
$lines | str join (char nl)
$cells | str join (char tab)
$names | str join ", "             # plain literal is fine for non-escape separators
```

### recursive globs: `**` matches the dir + all descendants (files AND dirs)

A `*` segment matches one level; `**` recurses to all depths. Build the
pattern by path-joining the components (don't concatenate a leading-`/`
literal - the hardcoded-path lint fires on it):

```nu
glob ($args.root | path join "**" "*")
```

Verified (nu 0.113.1): the result INCLUDES the root dir itself AND
returns files as well as directories (`**` and `**/*` behave the same).
So for "every subdirectory BELOW <root>", filter to dirs and drop the
root:

```nu
glob ($args.root | path join "**" "*")
| where {|p| (($p | path type) == "dir") and ($p != $args.root) }
```

## MCP Interface

### Overview
- [`run()`](#run) Evaluate a typed nushell source-code body on a stateless worker.
- [`interact()`](#interact) Evaluate a typed nushell source-code body on a persistent stateful worker.
- [`call()`](#call) Invoke a committed library function with typed args.
- [`rerun()`](#rerun) Re-evaluate a cached `run()` body with fresh args.
- [`processes()`](#processes) List in-flight MCP tool usage.
- [`kill()`](#kill) Cancel an in-flight usage by its nonce.
- [`info()`](#info) Versions, plugins, and libraries summary.
- [`inspect()`](#inspect) Detailed documentation of a specific callable library, module, function.
- [`new()`](#new) Scaffold a callable library / module / function into the agent's source-code repository.
- [`commit()`](#commit) Commit the agent's library source-code to the MCP's repository for live use.
- [`delete()`](#delete) Delete a library.
- [`learn()`](#learn) Generate the latest `/nu` SKILL.md.

### `run()`
*Evaluate a typed nushell source-code body on a stateless worker.*

#### arguments

Schema (partial):
```json
{
  "properties": {
    "args_schema":   { "type": "object", "additionalProperties": true },
    "result_schema": { "type": "object", "additionalProperties": true },
    "args":          { "type": "object", "additionalProperties": true },
    "body":          { "type": "string" },
    "timeout_ms":    { "type": ["integer", "null"], "format": "uint64", "minimum": 0 }
  },
  "required": ["args_schema", "result_schema", "args", "body"]
}
```

#### Example

Nu equivalent:
```nu
# @returns record<count: int, first: record<name: string, ratio: float>>
def run [args: record<eldest: bool, tabs: table<name: string, age: int, ratio: float>>] {
    let chosen = if $args.eldest {
        $args.tabs | sort-by age | last
    } else {
        $args.tabs | first
    }

    {
        count: ($args.tabs | length),
        first: {
            name: $chosen.name,
            ratio: $chosen.ratio
        }
    }
}
```

MCP (partial):
```json
{
  "arguments": {
    "args_schema": { "eldest": "bool", "tabs": [{ "name": "string", "age": "int", "ratio": "float" }] },
    "result_schema": { "count": "int", "first": { "name": "string", "ratio": "float" } },
    "args": {
      "eldest": true,
      "tabs": [
        {"name": "cindy", "age": 29, "ratio": 0.42},
        {"name": "bob",   "age": 32, "ratio": 0.77}
      ]
    },
    "body": "let chosen = if $args.eldest {\n    $args.tabs | sort-by age | last\n} else {\n    $args.tabs | first\n}\n\n{\n    count: ($args.tabs | length),\n    first: {\n        name: $chosen.name,\n        ratio: $chosen.ratio\n    }\n}"
  }
}
```

Output (partial):
```json
{
  "result": {
    "structuredContent": {
      "result": {
        "count": 2,
        "first": { "name": "bob", "ratio": 0.77 }
      },
      "nonce": "8jPtjMSfRVh",
      "rerun_id": "isaBZMrHho3"
    },
    "content": []
  }
}
```

### `interact()`
*Evaluate a typed nushell source-code body on a persistent stateful worker.*

Env mutations, `cd`, and top-level `def`s persist across calls; `run()`
state does not leak in. Use it for administrative, stateful sessions; use
`run()` for everything else.

#### arguments

Schema (partial):
```json
{
  "properties": {
    "args_schema":   { "type": "object", "additionalProperties": true },
    "result_schema": { "type": "object", "additionalProperties": true },
    "args":          { "type": "object", "additionalProperties": true },
    "body":          { "type": "string" },
    "timeout_ms":    { "type": ["integer", "null"], "format": "uint64", "minimum": 0 }
  },
  "required": ["args_schema", "result_schema", "args", "body"]
}
```

#### Example

Nu equivalent:
```nu
# @returns record<seeded: int>
def interact [args: record<value: int>] {
    $env.COUNTER = $args.value
    { seeded: $args.value }
}
```

MCP (partial):
```json
{
  "arguments": {
    "args_schema": { "value": "int" },
